CT.searchIndex = [{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s1","h":"How a C++ program is built","x":"How a C++ program is built C++ is a compiled language: your source code is translated into machine code before it runs. There is no interpreter watching over it, which makes C++ fast but also means the language trusts you not to make memory mistakes. #include // paste in the declarations for std::cout int main() { // execution starts here std::cout Preprocess : #include literally pastes header files in; #define does text substitution. Compile : each .cpp file becomes an object file on its own. Link : the linker joins object files and libraries into one executable and resolves calls between files. g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra main.cpp -o main ./main Habit to build now Always compile with -Wall -Wextra . The compiler warns about many of the bugs this lesson describes (uninitialized variables, unused values, signed/unsigned comparisons). Interview angle \"Compile error vs linker error?\" A compile error is a syntax or type problem inside one file. A linker error (\"undefined reference\") means a function was declared and called but never defined anywhere you linked."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s2","h":"Variables and types","x":"Variables and types Every variable has a type that fixes how much memory it uses and what you may do with it. Type Holds Typical size bool true / false 1 byte char a character / small integer 1 byte (always, by definition) int whole number 4 bytes long long bigger whole number 8 bytes float / double decimal number 4 / 8 bytes Sizes other than char are only guaranteed as minimums, so when size matters use the fixed-width types from <cstdint> : std::int32_t , std::uint64_t and so on. Initialization: always give a variable a value int a; // BAD: indeterminate value, reading it is undefined behavior int b = 5; // copy initialization int c(5); // direct initialization int d{5}; // brace (uniform) initialization: preferred int e{}; // empty braces: zero-initialized, e == 0 int f{3.7}; // ERROR: braces forbid narrowing double -> int auto g = 5; // g is int: the compiler deduces the type const int h = 9;// cannot be changed after initialization Undefined behavior A local variable you never initialize contains garbage left over in memory. Reading it is undefined behavior (UB) : the program may print junk, crash, or appear to work until the day it does not. The signed/unsigned trap unsigned types never go negative; they wrap around instead. Mixing signed and unsigned in a comparison converts the signed value to unsigned first. Predict What does this print? std::cout It prints 0 (false). -1 is converted to unsigned and becomes 4294967295, which is not less than 1. This is why -Wall -Wextra warns about sign comparisons, and why you should be careful comparing an int index with vector::size() (an unsigned type)."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s3","h":"Operators and control flow","x":"Operators and control flow 7 / 2 // 3 integer division truncates 7 % 2 // 1 remainder 7 / 2.0 // 3.5 one double operand makes the whole thing floating point if (x > 0 && y > 0) { } // && and || short-circuit: right side is skipped if not needed for (int i = 0; i Predict Prefix vs postfix int x = 5; int a = x++; // use x (5), then increment int b = ++x; // increment first, then use std::cout 5 7 7 x++ yields the old value and then increments, so a is 5 and x becomes 6. ++x increments first (x becomes 7) and yields the new value, so b is 7. Classic bug if (x = 5) assigns 5 to x and is always true. You meant == . The compiler warns with -Wall ."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s4","h":"Functions and passing arguments","x":"Functions and passing arguments A function must be declared before it is called. A declaration (prototype) says the name and types; the definition supplies the body. Headers hold declarations so many files can share them. int add(int a, int b); // declaration int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }// definition double add(double a, double b); // overload: same name, different parameter types void greet(std::string who = \"world\"); // default argument Three ways to pass a parameter void byValue(int n) { n++; } // works on a COPY void byRef(int& n) { n++; } // works on the ORIGINAL void byConstRef(const std::string& s) { } // original, read-only, no copy You want to... Use read a small value (int, double, char, pointer) by value: int n read a large object without copying it by const reference: const std::string& s modify the caller's variable by reference: int& n Predict What does this print? int a = 1; byValue(a); byRef(a); std::cout 2 . byValue incremented its own copy and threw it away. Only byRef changed a ."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s5","h":"Where variables live: stack, heap, static","x":"Where variables live: stack, heap, static Pointers only make sense once you know there are different regions of memory. Region What goes there Lifetime Speed / size Stack local variables, function parameters automatic: freed when the scope { } ends very fast, small (about 1 to 8 MB) Heap (free store) objects created with new / malloc manual: lives until you delete it slower, huge Static / global globals, static variables whole program fixed at startup int counter = 0; // static storage: exists for the whole program void f() { int local = 1; // stack: created on entry, destroyed on exit static int calls = 0; // static storage, but only visible inside f ++calls; int* p = new int(5); // the int lives on the HEAP; p itself is on the stack delete p; } Mental model The stack is a pile of plates: push one per function call, pop it when the call returns, everything on it vanishes automatically. The heap is a storage unit you rent: it stays yours until you hand the key back."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s6","h":"Pointers from scratch","x":"Pointers from scratch Every variable lives at an address in memory. A pointer is simply a variable whose value is an address. Syntax Meaning int* p declare p as \"pointer to int\" &x address-of: the address of x *p dereference: the thing p points to nullptr the pointer that points to nothing Step through this program and watch the memory. The addresses are made up for illustration. Pointers and arrays An array name decays to a pointer to its first element, and adding 1 to a pointer moves it by one element (not one byte). int a[3] = {10, 20, 30}; int* p = a; // same as &a[0] std::cout Careful Never dereference nullptr and never read past the end of an array. Both are undefined behavior. C++ will not stop you. Interview angle \"What is the difference between NULL and nullptr ?\" NULL is the integer 0 in disguise, so f(NULL) can call f(int) instead of f(char*) . nullptr has its own type and only converts to pointers."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s7","h":"References","x":"References A reference is an alias : another name for an existing variable. It is not a copy and it adds no new variable. int x = 1; int& r = x; // r is another name for x r = 5; // x is now 5 Pointer int* Reference int& Can be null yes no (must refer to something real) Must be initialized no (but should be) yes, at declaration Can be re-pointed yes no, bound for life Syntax to use *p , p->member just use the name Use for optional things, arrays, ownership, C APIs function parameters, aliases Predict The reference trap int a = 1, b = 2; int& r = a; r = b; std::cout 22 r = b does not make r refer to b . References cannot be re-bound. It assigns the value of b into the variable r aliases, so a becomes 2."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s8","h":"const and pointers","x":"const and pointers const means \"I promise not to modify this\". With pointers there are two things that can be const: the pointed-to value and the pointer itself. Read the declaration right to left. int x = 1, y = 2; const int* p1 = // \"p1 is a pointer to const int\" // *p1 = 5; // ERROR: cannot change the int through p1 p1 = // OK: the pointer itself may move int* const p2 = // \"p2 is a const pointer to int\" *p2 = 5; // OK: the int may change // p2 = // ERROR: the pointer may not move const int* const p3 = // neither can change Rule of thumb Whatever is to the left of the * is the pointed-to data; whatever is to the right is the pointer. const before the star protects the data. Make everything const that can be. It documents intent and lets the compiler catch mistakes. Prefer const T& parameters for big read-only objects."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s9","h":"Dynamic memory: new and delete","x":"Dynamic memory: new and delete new allocates on the heap and constructs the object, then returns a pointer. You must give the memory back with delete or it leaks . int* one = new int(42); delete one; // single object: new / delete int* arr = new int[100]; delete[] arr; // array: new[] / delete[] (must match!) Bug What happened Memory leak you lost the pointer (or forgot delete ) so the memory is never freed Dangling pointer you used a pointer after the memory was freed (see the step-through above) Double delete you freed the same memory twice: undefined behavior, often a crash Mismatch new[] freed with delete , or the reverse Modern C++ rule Avoid raw new and delete in new code. Use std::vector for arrays, and std::unique_ptr / std::make_unique for single objects: they free memory automatically. You will meet them in the Core Language lesson. Interview angle \"What does new do beyond malloc ?\" It calls the constructor, throws std::bad_alloc on failure (instead of returning null), and returns a correctly typed pointer. delete calls the destructor and then frees the memory."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s10","h":"Arrays, C-strings and std::string","x":"Arrays, C-strings and std::string int a[5] = {1, 2, 3}; // fixed size, remaining elements are 0, NO bounds checking a[10] = 7; // compiles, undefined behavior at runtime void f(int arr[]) { // this is really `int* arr`: the size is lost! sizeof(arr); // size of a POINTER (8), not of the array } char s[] = \"hi\"; // 3 chars: 'h', 'i', and a terminating '\\0' C-style arrays and strings are error-prone. In C++ you should almost always use: Instead of Use Why int a[N] std::array<int, N> knows its size, no decay, same speed new int[n] std::vector<int> grows, frees itself, .at(i) bounds-checks char* strings std::string owns its memory, safe copy, + , .size() , .find() std::string s = \"hello\"; s += \" world\"; // grows automatically std::cout v = {1, 2, 3}; v.push_back(4); // size is now 4"},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s11","h":"The classic memory bugs","x":"The classic memory bugs Most \"mysterious\" C++ crashes are one of these. Learn to spot them by eye. Bug Example Uninitialized read int n; use(n); Out-of-bounds int a[3]; a[3] = 1; (valid indices are 0 to 2) Null dereference int* p = nullptr; *p = 1; Use after free delete p; *p = 1; Signed overflow INT_MAX + 1 (UB for signed; wraps for unsigned) Returning a reference to a local int& bad() { int local = 42; return local; // BUG: local is destroyed when bad() returns } // the caller gets a dangling reference int good() { int local = 42; return local; // fine: returns a copy of the value } Tooling Compile with -fsanitize=address,undefined while testing. AddressSanitizer reports leaks, out-of-bounds access and use-after-free with the exact line; UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer catches overflow, bad shifts and more."},{"f":"01-basics.html","n":"C++ Basics and Pointers","id":"s12","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Try answering each one out loud before opening it. Pointer vs reference? A pointer holds an address, can be null, can be re-pointed, and needs * to use. A reference is a permanent alias that must be initialized and cannot be null. Use references for parameters, pointers when \"nothing\" is a valid value or you need to re-point. Stack vs heap? The stack holds locals and is freed automatically when the scope ends; it is fast but small. The heap holds new -allocated objects that live until you free them; it is large but slower and you must manage the lifetime (or let a smart pointer do it). What is a dangling pointer? A pointer to memory that has been freed or has gone out of scope. Dereferencing it is undefined behavior. Setting a pointer to nullptr after delete helps, and smart pointers avoid the problem. const int* vs int* const ? Read right to left. const int* is a pointer to a const int (data is read-only, pointer can move). int* const is a const pointer to an int (pointer is fixed, data can change). delete vs delete[] ? Memory from new must be freed with delete ; memory from new[] must be freed with delete[] so every element's destructor runs. Mixing them is undefined behavior. Deleting nullptr is safe and does nothing. What is undefined behavior? Code the standard places no requirements on: the compiler may assume it never happens, so anything can occur (crash, wrong output, or apparently fine). Examples: out-of-bounds access, null dereference, signed overflow, reading uninitialized memory, data races. How do you pass a large object to a function efficiently? By const reference if the function only reads it, to avoid a copy. By non-const reference if it must modify the caller's object. Keep going Next lesson: Object-Oriented Programming . Your own notes: Module 1: C++ Basics and the learncpp.com recaps (chapters 1 to 13 and 19). Reference: modern-cpp-features and the modern-cpp-tutorial ."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s1","h":"Classes and encapsulation","x":"Classes and encapsulation A class bundles data ( members ) with the functions that operate on it ( member functions , or methods). The key idea is encapsulation : keep the data private and expose only operations that keep it valid. class BankAccount { public: // the interface other code may use explicit BankAccount(double opening) : balance_(opening) {} bool withdraw(double amount) { if (amount balance_) return false; // invariant protected balance_ -= amount; return true; } double balance() const { return balance_; } private: // the implementation, hidden double balance_; }; BankAccount acc{100.0}; acc.withdraw(30); // fine // acc.balance_ = 1e9; // ERROR: private, nobody can break the invariant Specifier Who can access public everyone protected the class itself and classes derived from it private only the class itself (and its friends) struct vs class They are the same thing except for defaults: struct members and inheritance are public by default, class ones are private . By convention, use struct for plain data and class when there are invariants to protect."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s2","h":"Constructors and destructors","x":"Constructors and destructors A constructor runs when an object is created and sets it up. A destructor ( ~Name() ) runs automatically when the object's lifetime ends: at the closing brace for locals, or on delete for heap objects. This automatic cleanup is the foundation of RAII (see Core Language). class Widget { const int id_; std::string name_; public: Widget() : Widget(0) {} // delegating constructor Widget(int id, std::string n = \"none\") : id_(id), name_(std::move(n)) {} // member initializer list ~Widget() { /* release resources here */ } }; Use the initializer list (after the colon). It initializes members directly. Assigning in the body means the member was default-constructed first and then overwritten. const and reference members can only be set in the list. Members are initialized in declaration order , not in the order you write the list. Mismatched order is a classic warning ( -Wreorder ). explicit on a one-argument constructor stops the compiler converting an int into your type behind your back. Order of construction and destruction Base class first, then members (in declaration order), then the derived constructor body. Destruction is the exact reverse . Step through it: Interview angle \"In what order are constructors and destructors called with inheritance?\" Construct: base, members, derived body. Destruct: derived body, members (reverse), base. This is also why a derived class can safely use its base while its own body runs."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s3","h":"Copying and the Rule of 3, 5 and 0","x":"Copying and the Rule of 3, 5 and 0 If you do not write them, the compiler generates a copy constructor and copy assignment operator that copy every member one by one. For an int that is perfect. For a raw pointer it copies only the address , which is called a shallow copy , and that is a bug waiting to happen: The fix is a deep copy : give the class its own copy operations. This is the Rule of Three : if a class needs a custom destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment, it almost certainly needs all three. class Buffer { int* data_; public: Buffer(int v) : data_(new int(v)) {} ~Buffer() { delete data_; } // 1. destructor Buffer(const Buffer& o) : data_(new int(*o.data_)) {} // 2. copy constructor (deep) Buffer& operator=(const Buffer& o) { // 3. copy assignment (deep) if (this != &o) { *data_ = *o.data_; } // guard against a = a return *this; } }; Rule What you write When Rule of 0 none of them The goal. Hold resources in members that manage themselves: std::vector , std::string , std::unique_ptr . Rule of 3 destructor, copy ctor, copy assign The class owns a raw resource and must define copying. Rule of 5 the 3 plus move ctor and move assign Same, and you also want cheap moves (see Core Language). class NoCopy { public: NoCopy() = default; NoCopy(const NoCopy&) = delete; // forbid copying outright NoCopy& operator=(const NoCopy&) = delete; }; Best answer in an interview \"I follow the Rule of Zero: I use std::vector and smart pointers so the compiler-generated special members are already correct. I only write the Rule of Five when I wrap a raw resource.\""},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s4","h":"Member functions: this , const , static , friend","x":"Member functions: this , const , static , friend class Counter { int n_ = 0; static int instances_; // ONE copy shared by all Counter objects public: Counter() { ++instances_; } ~Counter() { --instances_; } Counter& add(int k) { n_ += k; return *this; } // `this` is a pointer to the current object int get() const { return n_; } // const: promises not to modify the object static int instances() { return instances_; } // no `this`: callable as Counter::instances() friend std::ostream& operator A const member function can be called on a const object, and the compiler stops it modifying members (except those marked mutable ). Make every getter const . A static member function belongs to the class, not an object, so it has no this . friend grants a function or class access to private members. Use it sparingly: it weakens encapsulation."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s5","h":"Inheritance","x":"Inheritance Public inheritance models \"is-a\" : a Dog is an Animal , so a Dog can be used anywhere an Animal is expected. class Animal { public: explicit Animal(std::string name) : name_(std::move(name)) {} const std::string& name() const { return name_; } protected: std::string name_; // visible to Dog, hidden from the outside world }; class Dog : public Animal { public: explicit Dog(std::string name) : Animal(std::move(name)) {} // call the base constructor void fetch() const { std::cout Object slicing Copying a derived object into a base value chops off the derived part: Animal a = Dog(\"Rex\"); keeps only the Animal bits, and virtual behaviour is lost. Pass and store polymorphic objects by pointer or reference , never by value. Mark a class final to forbid deriving from it. Prefer composition (see below) when the relationship is not truly is-a."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s6","h":"Polymorphism and virtual functions","x":"Polymorphism and virtual functions Polymorphism lets you call p->speak() through a base pointer and get the derived class's behaviour. It only works if the base function is virtual . Without virtual the compiler picks the function from the pointer's static type at compile time. With virtual it picks from the object's real dynamic type at runtime. Try it, and watch what changes: How it works: the vtable A class with virtual functions gets a hidden table of function pointers (the vtable ), one per class. Each object carries a hidden pointer to it (the vptr ), set up by the constructor. A virtual call is: follow the vptr, index the table, jump. The cost is one extra pointer per object and one indirection per call, and the call usually cannot be inlined. Term Meaning Overloading same name, different parameters, resolved at compile time Overriding a derived class replaces a virtual base function with the same signature, resolved at runtime Hiding a derived function with the same name (but not virtual / different signature) hides the base one struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; // ALWAYS virtual in a polymorphic base }; struct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {} double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; } // `override`: the compiler checks you really override }; Two famous traps 1. Always write override . If you mistype the signature (forget const ), without it you silently create a new function instead of overriding. 2. Virtual calls inside a constructor or destructor do not reach the derived class. While Base 's constructor runs, the object's dynamic type is Base , so Base::init() is called even if Derived overrides it."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s7","h":"Abstract classes and interfaces","x":"Abstract classes and interfaces A function declared = 0 is pure virtual . A class with at least one is abstract : you cannot create objects of it, only derive from it. A class with only pure virtual functions (and no data) is an interface . struct Logger { // interface virtual void log(const std::string& msg) = 0; virtual ~Logger() = default; }; struct ConsoleLogger : Logger { void log(const std::string& msg) override { std::cout > shapes; shapes.push_back(std::make_unique (2.0)); for (const auto& s : shapes) std::cout area()"},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s8","h":"Operator overloading","x":"Operator overloading You can give operators meaning for your own types so they read naturally. Do it only when the meaning is obvious ( + on vectors, == on values, << for printing). struct Vec2 { double x{}, y{}; Vec2& operator+=(const Vec2& o) { x += o.x; y += o.y; return *this; } // member: modifies *this bool operator==(const Vec2& o) const { return x == o.x && y == o.y; } }; Vec2 operator+(Vec2 a, const Vec2& b) { return a += b; } // non-member built from += std::ostream& operator Binary operators that do not change their operands ( + , == ) are best as non-member functions so both sides get implicit conversions; compound assignment ( += ) is a member. Keep the usual semantics: == and != together, < should be a strict weak ordering. C++20's operator<=> can generate all comparisons from one function. You cannot invent new operators or change precedence, and :: , . , .* , ?: cannot be overloaded."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s9","h":"Composition, relationships and design","x":"Composition, relationships and design Ask \"is it a, or does it have a?\" Inheritance is the strongest coupling in C++; prefer composition unless you truly need polymorphism. Relationship Meaning C++ shape Lifetime Inheritance (is-a) Dog is an Animal class Dog : public Animal one object Composition (owns) Car has an Engine, which dies with the car member Engine e; part dies with whole Aggregation (uses, shared) Team has Players who exist independently Player* / std::shared_ptr member independent Association Doctor treats Patients reference or pointer parameter independent Liskov Substitution Principle Code that works with a base type must keep working when handed any derived type. If Square : Rectangle breaks because setting the width also changes the height, inheritance was the wrong model. Public inheritance must mean \"behaves like\", not just \"shares code\". Related notes in your repo: Module 2: C++ OOP , objects relationships , and the learncpp.com recaps for chapters 14, 15, 21, 23, 24 and 25."},{"f":"02-oop.html","n":"Object-Oriented Programming in C++","id":"s10","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Why must a base class destructor be virtual? If you delete a derived object through a base pointer and the destructor is not virtual, only the base destructor runs: undefined behavior, and the derived part's resources leak. A virtual destructor makes delete dispatch to the most derived destructor first. Any class meant to be a polymorphic base needs one. What is the difference between overloading and overriding? Overloading: several functions with the same name and different parameters in the same scope, chosen at compile time. Overriding: a derived class provides its own implementation of a base virtual function with the same signature, chosen at runtime. What is a pure virtual function and an abstract class? A pure virtual function is declared = 0 and has no required implementation. A class with at least one cannot be instantiated; it defines an interface that derived classes must implement. What is the Rule of Three / Five / Zero? Three: if you define a destructor, copy constructor or copy assignment, define all three. Five: add the move constructor and move assignment. Zero: design classes so none are needed by holding resources in self-managing members like vector and unique_ptr . What is object slicing? Copying a derived object into a base-class value discards the derived members and virtual behaviour. Avoid it by passing polymorphic objects by reference or pointer. What does explicit do? It stops a constructor (or conversion operator) from being used for implicit conversions. Without it, void f(Account); f(5); could silently build an Account from 5. Can a constructor be virtual? Can a destructor be pure virtual? A constructor cannot be virtual: the vptr is set up by the constructor, so there is no dynamic type yet. A destructor can be pure virtual to make a class abstract, but it still needs a body because derived destructors call it. Composition or inheritance? Prefer composition. Use public inheritance only for a true is-a relationship where you need runtime polymorphism, and make sure it satisfies Liskov substitution."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s1","h":"RAII: the central idea","x":"RAII: the central idea Resource Acquisition Is Initialization. Tie the lifetime of a resource (heap memory, file, lock, socket) to the lifetime of an object. Acquire it in the constructor, release it in the destructor. Since destructors run automatically at the end of scope, even when an exception is thrown , the resource can never leak. class File { std::FILE* f_; public: explicit File(const char* path) : f_(std::fopen(path, \"r\")) {} // acquire ~File() { if (f_) std::fclose(f_); } // release File(const File&) = delete; // one owner only File& operator=(const File&) = delete; }; Watch what happens when something throws in the middle: You have already been using RAII: std::vector frees its memory, std::unique_ptr deletes its object, std::lock_guard unlocks its mutex, std::fstream closes its file. The rule: never manage a resource with a bare new / delete , lock() / unlock() or fopen / fclose pair in your own code. Wrap it. Interview angle \"What is RAII and why is it important?\" It binds resource lifetime to object lifetime so cleanup is automatic and exception-safe. It replaces try/finally (which C++ does not have) and is the reason C++ needs no garbage collector."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s2","h":"Lvalues, rvalues and references","x":"Lvalues, rvalues and references An lvalue is an object with a name and an address you can take ( x , v[0] ). An rvalue is a temporary with no name ( 5 , a + b , a function's returned value). Rvalues are about to disappear, so their guts are safe to steal. Reference type Binds to Typical use T& lvalues only modify the caller's object const T& lvalues and rvalues read-only, avoid a copy T&& (rvalue reference) rvalues only move constructors and move assignment void f(const std::string& s); // accepts anything, copies nothing void g(std::string&& s); // accepts only temporaries: \"I may take your contents\" std::string name = \"abc\"; f(name); // OK // g(name); // ERROR: name is an lvalue g(std::string(\"x\")); // OK: temporary g(std::move(name)); // OK: std::move says \"treat name as a temporary\" A name is always an lvalue Inside g , the parameter s has a name, so it is an lvalue there even though its type is std::string&& . To pass it on as an rvalue again you must write std::move(s) (or std::forward in templates)."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s3","h":"Move semantics","x":"Move semantics Copying a std::vector with a million elements allocates and copies a million elements. If the source is a temporary that is about to be destroyed anyway, that is wasteful. A move just steals the internal pointer and leaves the source empty. std::move(x) does not move anything . It is only a cast to an rvalue reference, a signal that says \"you may steal from this\". The actual stealing happens in the move constructor or move assignment that gets selected. A moved-from object is valid but unspecified . You may destroy it or assign a new value to it. Do not read its value. If your type holds a raw resource, write the Rule of Five. Mark the move operations noexcept : std::vector only moves elements during reallocation if the move cannot throw, otherwise it falls back to copying. class Buffer { int* data_; std::size_t n_; public: Buffer(Buffer&& o) noexcept : data_(o.data_), n_(o.n_) { // steal o.data_ = nullptr; o.n_ = 0; // leave source safely empty } Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& o) noexcept { if (this != &o) { delete[] data_; data_ = o.data_; n_ = o.n_; o.data_ = nullptr; o.n_ = 0; } return *this; } /* ... constructor, destructor, copy operations ... */ }; Buffer make() { Buffer b(1000); return b; } // fine: NO std::move needed on return Do not write return std::move(local); Returning a local by value already allows the compiler to elide the copy entirely (RVO/NRVO) or move it automatically. Adding std::move blocks the elision and makes things slower."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s4","h":"Smart pointers","x":"Smart pointers Smart pointers are RAII wrappers around new / delete in <memory> . They make ownership explicit in the type. Type Ownership Copyable? Cost std::unique_ptr<T> exclusive: one owner no , only movable same as a raw pointer std::shared_ptr<T> shared: reference counted, freed when the last owner goes yes (bumps the count) extra control block, atomic count std::weak_ptr<T> none: observes a shared_ptr without keeping it alive yes small auto p = std::make_unique (1, \"a\"); // preferred over `new` // auto q = p; // ERROR: unique_ptr cannot be copied auto q = std::move(p); // OK: ownership moves, p is now null auto s1 = std::make_shared (2, \"b\"); // one allocation for object + control block auto s2 = s1; // two owners, use_count() == 2 std::weak_ptr w = s1; // observe only if (auto sp = w.lock()) { /* still alive: use sp */ } // lock() gives a shared_ptr or null Step through the lifetime of a shared object: Reference cycles and weak_ptr If A holds a shared_ptr<B> and B holds a shared_ptr<A> , each keeps the other's count above zero forever: neither is ever destroyed , a leak that RAII cannot fix. Make one direction (usually the \"back-pointer\" or parent link) a weak_ptr . Ownership rules of thumb Default to unique_ptr (or better, a plain value member). Use shared_ptr only when ownership is truly shared. A raw pointer or a reference means \"I use it, I do not own it\". Pass a smart pointer by value only to transfer or share ownership; otherwise pass T& or T* . Interview angle \"Why make_unique / make_shared instead of new ?\" No naked new (exception safety, no leak if another argument throws), less typing, and make_shared does a single allocation instead of two."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s5","h":"Modern syntax tour","x":"Modern syntax tour These are the C++11/14/17 features you are expected to use naturally. Each replaces an older, more error-prone idiom. Feature Instead of Example auto long type names auto it = m.find(k); Range-based for index / iterator loops for (const auto& x : v) {} nullptr NULL / 0 int* p = nullptr; enum class plain enum enum class Color { Red, Green }; scoped, no implicit int using alias typedef using Map = std::map<int, std::string>; Brace init constructor parens std::vector<int> v{1, 2, 3}; override / final hoping you matched the signature void f() override; = default / = delete empty bodies, private unimplemented copy Foo(const Foo&) = delete; constexpr macros, template tricks constexpr int sq(int x) { return x*x; } static_assert runtime checks of compile-time facts static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4); Structured bindings (C++17) .first / .second for (auto& [key, val] : m) {} if with initializer (C++17) variable leaking into outer scope if (auto it = m.find(k); it != m.end()) {} [[nodiscard]] ignored error codes [[nodiscard]] bool open(); auto traps auto x = {1, 2, 3}; deduces std::initializer_list<int> , not a vector. auto drops references and const : auto y = v[0]; copies the element; write auto& y or const auto& y to avoid the copy. Likewise for (auto [k, v] : map) copies every pair; use const auto& ."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s6","h":"Lambdas","x":"Lambdas A lambda is an unnamed function object you write in place. The compiler turns it into a small class whose operator() holds the body and whose members hold the captures. // [captures] (parameters) -> return_type { body } auto add = [](int a, int b) { return a + b; }; add(2, 3); // 5 std::vector v{5, 2, 9, 1}; std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a > b; }); // descending int factor = 3; auto byValue = [factor](int x) { return x * factor; }; // copies factor now auto byRef = [&factor](int x) { return x * factor; }; // refers to the live variable auto all = [=] (int x) { return x * factor; }; // [=] all by value, [&] all by reference auto generic = [](const auto& a, const auto& b) { return a Predict Capture by value vs by reference int x = 1; auto byVal = [x] { return x; }; auto byRef = [&x] { return x; }; x = 2; std::cout 12 byVal took a snapshot of x (1) when it was created. byRef sees the variable itself, so it reads the new value 2. A by-value capture is const inside the lambda unless you add mutable . Dangling captures A lambda that captures a local by reference and then outlives it (returned from the function, or run later on another thread) reads destroyed memory. When a lambda escapes its scope, capture by value. std::function can store any callable of a given signature, but it type-erases and may allocate. Prefer auto for a local lambda and a template parameter for a function that accepts one."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s7","h":"Exceptions and exception safety","x":"Exceptions and exception safety double divide(double a, double b) { if (b == 0) throw std::invalid_argument(\"division by zero\"); return a / b; } try { divide(1, 0); } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) { // catch by const reference std::cerr Throw by value, catch by const& . Catching by value slices the exception object. When a throw happens, the runtime performs stack unwinding : it leaves scopes one by one, running destructors, until a matching catch is found. This is why RAII makes code exception-safe. A destructor must never throw . If it throws during unwinding, std::terminate is called and the program dies. Destructors are implicitly noexcept . noexcept promises a function will not throw. It enables optimisations (such as moves in vector ) and if the promise is broken the program terminates. Use exceptions for exceptional failure, not routine control flow, and not on real-time hot paths where the throw cost is unacceptable. Exception safety level Promise No-throw never throws ( noexcept ): swap, move, destructors Strong if it throws, state is exactly as before (commit-or-rollback) Basic if it throws, nothing leaks and invariants hold, but values may have changed"},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s8","h":"Casts and type conversion","x":"Casts and type conversion C++ replaces the single dangerous C cast (T)x with four named casts, each saying exactly what you intend and easy to search for. Cast Use for Danger static_cast<T>(x) well-defined conversions: int to double , base to derived pointer when you are sure low dynamic_cast<T>(x) checked downcast in a polymorphic hierarchy. Returns nullptr (pointer) or throws std::bad_cast (reference) on failure runtime cost; base needs a virtual function const_cast<T>(x) add or remove const (legacy APIs) modifying a truly const object is UB reinterpret_cast<T>(x) reinterpret raw bits (pointer to integer, unrelated pointer types) very high, rarely correct double avg = static_cast (total) / count; // avoids integer division Animal* a = getAnimal(); if (auto* d = dynamic_cast (a)) { // safe downcast: null if it is not a Dog d->fetch(); } Rule If you need many casts, the design is probably wrong. Prefer virtual functions to dynamic_cast chains, and never use a C-style cast in new code."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s9","h":"const , constexpr and static","x":"const , constexpr and static const : the value cannot change after initialization (checked at compile time). Use it everywhere you can. constexpr : the value (or function result) can be computed at compile time , so it costs nothing at runtime and can be used in array sizes and template arguments. constexpr int square(int x) { return x * x; } constexpr int N = square(4); // 16, computed by the compiler std::array a; // size 9: legal because it is a constant expression int runtime = readInput(); int r = square(runtime); // also fine: same function runs at runtime static_assert(N == 16, \"math is broken\"); // checked at compile time The keyword static means three different things depending on where it appears: Where Meaning Local variable in a function one instance that lives for the whole program and keeps its value between calls Global / namespace scope internal linkage: visible only inside this .cpp file Class member belongs to the class, shared by all objects"},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s10","h":"What C++14, 17 and 20 added","x":"What C++14, 17 and 20 added Interviewers often ask \"what modern features do you use?\". Know at least these, and why they help. Standard Feature One-liner C++11 move semantics, smart pointers, lambdas, auto , constexpr , std::thread , variadic templates, nullptr , range-for The big modernisation. C++14 generic lambdas, make_unique , return type deduction, binary literals 0b101 Polishes C++11. C++17 std::optional , std::variant , std::string_view , structured bindings, if constexpr , fold expressions, std::filesystem , parallel algorithms, std::scoped_lock , CTAD Vocabulary types and compile-time branching. C++20 concepts, ranges, std::span , operator<=> , coroutines, modules, std::jthread , std::format Constraints on templates, composable algorithms. std::optional parse(const std::string& s); // \"maybe an int\" without magic values if (auto n = parse(\"42\")) { use(*n); } std::variant v = \"hi\"; // type-safe union std::visit([](auto&& x) { std::cout For the full list with examples see the modern-cpp-features cheat sheet (C++11, 14, 17, 20, 23) and the modern-cpp-tutorial book. Your own notes: Module 3 and learncpp.com chapters 10, 12, 19, 22, 26 and 27."},{"f":"03-core-language.html","n":"Core Language and Modern C++","id":"s11","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet What does std::move actually do? Nothing at runtime: it is an unconditional cast to an rvalue reference. It enables the move constructor or move assignment to be chosen. The moved-from object stays valid but unspecified. unique_ptr vs shared_ptr vs weak_ptr ? unique_ptr : sole owner, move-only, zero overhead. shared_ptr : reference-counted shared ownership with a control block and atomic count. weak_ptr : non-owning observer used to break cycles and to check whether a shared object is still alive ( lock() ). Is shared_ptr thread-safe? The reference count is atomic, so copying and destroying shared_ptr s from different threads is safe. The pointed-to object is not protected: concurrent access to it still needs synchronization. Concurrently modifying the same shared_ptr variable is also a race. What is copy elision / RVO? The compiler constructs a returned object directly in the caller's storage, skipping the copy or move. In C++17 it is guaranteed when returning a temporary (prvalue). Do not write return std::move(local) : it prevents it. What is stack unwinding? When an exception propagates, the runtime exits scopes up the call stack, calling the destructors of all fully constructed locals until a handler is found. It is what makes RAII exception-safe. Why should destructors not throw? Destructors run during unwinding. If one throws while another exception is active, std::terminate is called. Destructors are noexcept by default; catch and handle errors inside them. When would you use dynamic_cast ? For a checked downcast from a polymorphic base to a derived type when you cannot express the need through a virtual function. It returns null (pointers) or throws bad_cast (references) on failure. Frequent use suggests a design problem. Difference between const and constexpr ? const means read-only after initialization (the value may be determined at runtime). constexpr means the value or function can be evaluated at compile time and usable in constant expressions."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s1","h":"The big picture","x":"The big picture The STL has three cooperating parts: Part What it is Examples Containers classes that own and organise a collection of elements vector , map , set , deque Iterators generalised pointers that walk a container v.begin() , m.find(k) Algorithms generic functions that operate on iterator ranges sort , find , accumulate Algorithms never touch containers directly. They only see a pair of iterators [first, last) , so one std::sort works on a vector, an array or a deque, and one container can be used with hundreds of algorithms. That separation is the design genius of the library. #include // std::vector #include // std::map, std::multimap #include // std::unordered_map #include // std::set #include // std::sort, std::find, std::transform ... #include // std::accumulate, std::iota #include // std::string Default choice If you do not have a reason to pick something else, use std::vector . Contiguous memory is so cache-friendly that it often beats \"theoretically better\" containers for realistic sizes."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s2","h":"vector : the workhorse","x":"vector : the workhorse A vector stores its elements in one contiguous heap array. It tracks two numbers: size (elements in use) and capacity (slots allocated). When push_back finds no spare capacity it allocates a bigger buffer, moves or copies every element across , and frees the old one. That is O(n) once in a while, but because capacity grows geometrically the average cost per push_back is amortised O(1) . std::vector v; v.reserve(100); // capacity 100, size still 0: no reallocation for the next 100 push_backs v.push_back(1); // copy/move an existing object in v.emplace_back(2); // construct the element in place: no temporary v.resize(10); // size becomes 10; new elements are value-initialised (0) v[3]; // no bounds check: out-of-range is undefined behavior v.at(3); // bounds-checked: throws std::out_of_range v.front(); v.back(); v.data(); // data() is the raw int* for C APIs v.size(); v.capacity(); v.empty(); reserve vs resize reserve(n) only allocates space (capacity). resize(n) changes the number of elements (size). Calling v[5] = x after only reserve(10) is undefined behavior: element 5 does not exist yet."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s3","h":"Other sequence containers","x":"Other sequence containers Container Storage Best at Watch out std::array<T,N> fixed size, inline (stack) size known at compile time; zero overhead cannot grow std::vector<T> one contiguous heap block almost everything; fast iteration and indexing insert/erase in the middle is O(n) std::deque<T> chunks of arrays fast push/pop at both ends, still indexable not contiguous std::list<T> doubly linked nodes O(1) insert/erase anywhere if you hold an iterator ; iterators stay valid no indexing, a heap allocation per element, poor cache behaviour std::forward_list<T> singly linked nodes minimal memory list forward iteration only Operation vector deque list index [i] O(1) O(1) not available push_back amortised O(1) O(1) O(1) push_front O(n) O(1) O(1) insert / erase in the middle O(n) O(n) O(1) at a known position find a value O(n) O(n) O(n)"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s4","h":"Associative and unordered containers","x":"Associative and unordered containers map / set unordered_map / unordered_set Implementation balanced binary tree (usually red-black) hash table Order of iteration sorted by key unspecified find / insert / erase O(log n) average O(1), worst case O(n) Key requirement operator< (a strict weak ordering) a hash function and operator== Range queries ( lower_bound ) yes no std::map age; age[\"Ana\"] = 30; // operator[] INSERTS a default (0) if the key is missing age.insert({\"Bob\", 25}); // does nothing if \"Bob\" already exists age.insert_or_assign(\"Bob\", 26); // C++17: insert or overwrite if (auto it = age.find(\"Ana\"); it != age.end()) // find never inserts std::cout second; // it->first is the key, it->second the value for (const auto& [name, years] : age) // structured bindings; sorted by name std::cout seen; if (!seen.insert(5).second) { /* second == false means it was already there */ } Predict Does looking up make the map bigger? std::map m; if (m[\"x\"] == 0) { } std::cout 1 operator[] on a missing key inserts it with a default-constructed value. To only check, use m.find(\"x\") , m.count(\"x\") or m.contains(\"x\") (C++20). Container adaptors They wrap another container and restrict the interface: std::stack (LIFO: push/pop/top ), std::queue (FIFO: push/pop/front ) and std::priority_queue (a heap: top() is the largest element by default). std::priority_queue maxHeap; // largest first std::priority_queue , std::greater > minHeap; // smallest first"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s5","h":"Which container should I use?","x":"Which container should I use? Pick what you mostly do. The advisor gives the container and the reason. Interview angle \"vector vs list?\" Answer with memory , not just big-O: a vector is contiguous, so traversals are cache-friendly and in practice beat a list even for inserts in the middle at modest sizes. A list wins only when you need stable iterators/references and cheap splicing."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s6","h":"Iterators","x":"Iterators An iterator behaves like a pointer: *it reads the element, ++it moves on, it == end ends the walk. Every container exposes begin() (first element) and end() ( one past the last ). Ranges are half-open : [begin, end) . std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) std::cout Category Can do Containers Forward ++ , read (multi-pass) forward_list , unordered containers Bidirectional also -- list , map , set Random access also it + n , it[i] , it1 - it2 , < vector , deque , array , string This is why std::sort needs random-access iterators and does not work on std::list (which has its own member list.sort() ). Iterator invalidation Changing a container can make existing iterators, pointers and references invalid . Using one is undefined behavior. Memorise this table: Container Operation What is invalidated vector push_back , insert all if it reallocated; otherwise those at or after the insertion point vector erase the erased element and everything after it deque insert/erase in the middle everything; push at either end invalidates iterators but not references list , map , set insert nothing list , map , set erase only the erased element unordered_* insert that triggers a rehash all iterators (references stay valid) Spot the bug Erasing inside a loop std::vector v{1, 2, 3, 4}; for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) if (*it % 2 == 0) v.erase(it); // BUG erase invalidates it , and the loop then does ++it on a dead iterator. The correct idiom uses the iterator erase returns: for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) { if (*it % 2 == 0) it = v.erase(it); // erase returns the next valid iterator else ++it; } // or, in C++20: std::erase_if(v, [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; });"},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s7","h":"Algorithms","x":"Algorithms The <algorithm> and <numeric> headers hold over a hundred generic functions. Prefer them to hand-written loops: they are named (so they document intent), tested, and often faster. Most take a predicate or comparator , usually a lambda. Try some on the array {5, 2, 9, 2, 7, 4} : std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](const P& a, const P& b) { return a.age 6; }); int n = std::count_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x % 2 == 0; }); bool any = std::any_of(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) { return x std::transform(v.begin(), v.end(), v.begin(), [](int x) { return x * x; }); auto pos = std::lower_bound(sorted.begin(), sorted.end(), 5); // binary search: range MUST be sorted Predict The remove surprise std::vector v{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}; std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3); std::cout 5 Algorithms only see iterators, so they cannot change a container's size. std::remove shifts the kept elements forward and returns the new logical end, leaving junk after it. To really remove, use the erase-remove idiom : v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3), v.end()); // classic std::erase(v, 3); // C++20: one call Comparators must be a strict weak ordering Use < , never <= . A comparator that returns true for equal elements violates the contract and can crash std::sort or corrupt a std::set . std::sort is O(n log n) and not stable; use std::stable_sort to preserve the order of equal elements."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s8","h":"Strings and string_view","x":"Strings and string_view std::string s = \"hello world\"; s.size(); s.empty(); s[0]; s.at(0); s += \"!\"; // append s.substr(0, 5); // \"hello\" auto pos = s.find(\"world\"); // index, or std::string::npos if not found if (pos != std::string::npos) { } std::to_string(42); // \"42\" std::stoi(\"123\"); // 123 (throws std::invalid_argument on bad input) std::string_view sv = s; // non-owning view: no allocation, no copy void log(std::string_view msg); // accepts string, literal and substring views cheaply std::string stores small strings (about 15 chars with common implementations) inline without a heap allocation: the small string optimisation. string_view must never outlive the string it points into. Returning a string_view to a local string, or storing one from a temporary, dangles. Use std::stringstream / std::getline for parsing and formatting streams."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s9","h":"Useful companions and pitfalls","x":"Useful companions and pitfalls std::pair p{1, \"a\"}; p.first; p.second; auto t = std::make_tuple(1, 2.5, \"x\"); std::get (t); // 2.5 auto [id, name] = p; // structured binding (C++17) std::optional maybe = std::nullopt; // \"value or nothing\" std::array a{1, 2, 3}; // fixed-size, knows its size Pitfall Fix Using an iterator/pointer/reference after a push_back reallocated the vector reserve up front, or re-fetch the iterator, or index instead map[key] silently inserts use find / contains to test; at() to read-or-throw v.size() - 1 on an empty vector ( size() is unsigned, so it wraps to a huge number) check empty() first or write i + 1 < v.size() Passing a big container by value pass const std::vector<T>& std::vector<bool> is a packed-bit special case with proxy references use std::vector<char> or std::bitset auto x = v[0] copies the element const auto& x = v[0] std::find on a map is O(n) use the member m.find(key) , O(log n) More in your notes: Module 3: C++ Standard Library (containers, iterators, algorithms, strings) and learncpp.com chapters 16, 17 and 18."},{"f":"04-stl.html","n":"The C++ Standard Template Library","id":"s10","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet How does std::vector grow? When size == capacity it allocates a larger buffer (geometric growth, typically 1.5x to 2x, implementation-defined), moves or copies the elements, and frees the old buffer. This makes push_back amortised O(1), but it invalidates all iterators, pointers and references. reserve avoids the reallocations. map vs unordered_map ? map : balanced tree, sorted iteration, O(log n), needs operator< , supports range queries. unordered_map : hash table, average O(1) (worst O(n) with many collisions), no order, needs a hash and == . Choose unordered_map for plain lookups and map when you need order. push_back vs emplace_back ? push_back takes an existing object (a copy or move). emplace_back forwards its arguments to the element's constructor and builds it in place, avoiding a temporary. For simple types there is no difference; for objects with expensive construction emplace_back can save a move. What is the erase-remove idiom? std::remove / remove_if only moves the elements to keep to the front and returns the new end; it cannot shrink the container. Pass that to the container's erase to actually delete the tail: v.erase(std::remove_if(...), v.end()) . C++20 has std::erase_if . What is a hash collision and how is it handled? Two different keys hash to the same bucket. The standard library's unordered containers resolve it with chaining (a bucket holds a list of entries), so lookups in a heavily colliding bucket degrade toward O(n). A good hash function and a sensible load factor ( rehash , reserve ) keep it near O(1). Why can't I std::sort a std::list ? sort needs random-access iterators (it jumps around by index). A list only provides bidirectional iterators, so it has its own member function list::sort . Which container for a task queue? For \"top K\" elements? A queue ( std::queue , backed by deque ) for FIFO tasks. A priority_queue (heap) for top K: keep a heap of size K and compare against its root: O(n log K)."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s1","h":"Why templates, and function templates","x":"Why templates, and function templates Without templates you would write maxInt , maxDouble , maxString ... identical except for the type. A template is a blueprint with the type as a parameter: template T maxOf(T a, T b) { return a (3, 2.5);// explicit: T = double, the int is converted The compiler does not compile the template itself. Each time you use it with a new type it instantiates it: stamps out a real function with T replaced, then compiles that. This is compile-time polymorphism : no runtime overhead, but the code is generated per type (more binary size) and errors only appear when a bad type is used. Pick a type and see what the compiler does with maxOf : Interview angle \"Templates vs virtual functions?\" Templates resolve at compile time (static polymorphism): fastest, can be inlined, but every instantiation is separate code and types must be known at compile time. Virtual functions resolve at runtime : one piece of code, objects chosen dynamically, but a vtable indirection per call."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s2","h":"Class templates","x":"Class templates The same idea for classes: std::vector<T> , std::map<K,V> , std::unique_ptr<T> are all class templates. template // a type parameter, a NON-TYPE parameter, with a default class Stack { std::array items_; std::size_t size_ = 0; public: bool push(const T& v) { if (size_ == Capacity) return false; items_[size_++] = v; return true; } T pop() { return items_[--size_]; } bool empty() const { return size_ == 0; } }; Stack a; // Stack Stack b; // a different, unrelated type std::pair p{1, 2.5}; // C++17 CTAD: class template argument deduction -> std::pair std::vector v{1, 2, 3}; // std::vector Non-type parameters ( std::size_t Capacity ) are compile-time constants, which is how std::array<int, 5> knows its size. Each instantiation ( Stack<int> , Stack<double> ) is a completely separate class with no relationship to the others. Member functions of a class template are only instantiated if you actually call them. Why templates live in headers The compiler needs the full definition at the point of instantiation. If you put a template's body in a .cpp file, other files see only the declaration and the linker reports undefined references. Put templates in headers (or use explicit instantiation template class Stack<int>; in the .cpp )."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s3","h":"Specialization","x":"Specialization Sometimes one type deserves a different implementation. A specialization overrides the generic template for specific arguments. template struct Printer { // primary template static void print(const T& v) { std::cout // full specialization for bool struct Printer { static void print(bool v) { std::cout struct Printer { // PARTIAL specialization: any pointer type static void print(T* p) { std::cout Kind Meaning Allowed for Full specialization all parameters fixed: template<> ... Printer<bool> class and function templates Partial specialization a pattern: Printer<T*> class templates only For functions, prefer overloads You cannot partially specialize a function template. Overload it instead ( void f(int) next to template<class T> void f(T) ): overload resolution prefers the non-template when it matches exactly. Function template specializations also do not take part in overload resolution, which surprises people."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s4","h":"Deduction and forwarding references","x":"Deduction and forwarding references How T is deduced depends on how the parameter is written. The most important case is T&& in a function template : it is not an rvalue reference, it is a forwarding reference (also called a universal reference) that binds to anything and remembers whether it was an lvalue or rvalue. Explore the rules: Reference collapsing Deducing T = int& for T&& would mean a \"reference to reference\", which C++ resolves with a simple rule: if either is an lvalue reference, the result is an lvalue reference; only && + && stays an rvalue reference. Written Collapses to T& & , T& && , T&& & T& T&& && T&& Perfect forwarding Problem: a wrapper function takes arguments and passes them on to another function. Inside the wrapper the parameter has a name , so it is an lvalue, and the callee would always copy it. std::forward restores the original value category: template std::unique_ptr makeUnique(Args&&... args) { // forwarding references return std::unique_ptr (new T(std::forward (args)...)); // lvalues stay lvalues, rvalues stay rvalues } std::string s = \"abc\"; auto a = makeUnique (s); // s is an lvalue -> Widget copies it auto b = makeUnique (std::string(\"x\"));// temporary rvalue -> Widget MOVES it std::move vs std::forward std::move(x) always casts to an rvalue (\"steal from this\"). std::forward<T>(x) casts to an rvalue only if the original argument was an rvalue, and is used with forwarding references. Only use forward on a forwarding reference, and use it once."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s5","h":"Variadic templates and fold expressions","x":"Variadic templates and fold expressions A parameter pack ( typename... Args ) lets a template take any number of arguments. You expand the pack with args... . Before C++17 you processed a pack by recursion: peel off the first element, recurse on the rest, and stop with a base case. template T sum(T v) { return v; } // base case: one argument template T sum(T first, Rest... rest) { return first + sum(rest...); } // peel one off, recurse sum(1, 2, 3); Watch the compiler instantiate a chain of real functions: C++17 fold expressions replace the recursion with one line: template auto sum(Args... args) { return (args + ...); } // unary right fold: a + (b + (c ...)) template void print(Args&&... args) { ((std::cout Variadics are behind std::make_unique , std::make_shared , emplace_back , std::tuple , std::thread and std::function ."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s6","h":"Type traits and if constexpr","x":"Type traits and if constexpr <type_traits> lets code ask questions about types at compile time and even build new types from them. #include std::is_integral_v ; // true (C++17: _v suffix gives the bool directly) std::is_floating_point_v ; // true std::is_same_v ; // true std::is_pointer_v ; // true std::is_base_of_v ; // true std::remove_reference_t ; // int (_t suffix gives the type directly) std::decay_t ; // int std::conditional_t 2), long, short>; // long if constexpr (C++17): compile-time branching template std::string describe(const T& v) { if constexpr (std::is_integral_v ) return \"integer \" + std::to_string(v); else if constexpr (std::is_floating_point_v ) return \"float \" + std::to_string(v); else return \"something else\"; } The condition is evaluated while compiling and the untaken branches are discarded, not even compiled , so each branch may use operations that are invalid for the other types. A normal if would fail to compile as soon as one branch did not make sense for T . SFINAE and enable_if (the older way) S ubstitution F ailure I s N ot A n E rror: if substituting a type into a template's signature produces invalid code, that overload is silently dropped from consideration instead of causing a compile error. template >> T twice(T x) { return x * 2; } twice(21); // OK // twice(2.5); // the overload is removed: \"no matching function\" error Concepts (C++20): the modern replacement template // or: template requires std::integral T twice(T x) { return x * 2; } // twice(2.5); // error: \"constraint not satisfied: double is not integral\": a readable message"},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s7","h":"Compile-time computation","x":"Compile-time computation Templates are accidentally a compile-time programming language (template metaprogramming). The classic example computes a factorial during compilation using recursion on the template argument: template struct Fact { static constexpr unsigned long value = N * Fact ::value; }; template <> struct Fact { static constexpr unsigned long value = 1; }; // specialization ends the recursion static_assert(Fact ::value == 120); // evaluated by the compiler, zero runtime cost Modern C++ does the same far more readably with constexpr functions, which look like ordinary code: constexpr unsigned long fact(unsigned n) { return n a; // size 24, computed at compile time Use constexpr for values and if constexpr , concepts and type traits for decisions; reach for hand-written template recursion only in older code or when a type must be computed."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s8","h":"Pitfalls and good practice","x":"Pitfalls and good practice Pitfall Explanation / fix Huge unreadable error messages The error is reported deep inside an instantiation. Read the first error and the line \"required from here\". Concepts or static_assert on traits give early, clear errors. Template body in a .cpp file Linker errors. Put the definition in the header. typename for dependent types Inside a template, T::iterator could be a type or a value. Write typename T::iterator it; . Members of a dependent base class not found Write this->member or Base<T>::member . Code bloat Every distinct T generates more machine code. Move type-independent logic into a non-template helper. Deduction conflicts: maxOf(3, 2.5) Two different deduced T s fail. Cast, specify maxOf<double> , or use two parameters. maxOf(\"a\", \"b\") with const char* Compares pointer addresses, not text. Use std::string or std::string_view . See also your notes in Module 4: Templates and Design Patterns (templates, type traits, metaprogramming, variadic templates, perfect forwarding) and learncpp.com chapters 11 and 26, plus the modern-cpp-features sections on variadic templates, forwarding references, fold expressions, CTAD and if constexpr ."},{"f":"05-templates.html","n":"C++ Templates","id":"s9","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet What happens when you use a function template? The compiler deduces the template arguments from the call, then instantiates a concrete function for that combination of types (once per distinct combination across the program), and compiles it like normal code. The template itself generates no code. What is a forwarding reference? A T&& parameter where T is deduced for that very function template (or auto&& ). It binds to lvalues and rvalues alike: for an lvalue T becomes X& and reference collapsing makes the parameter X& ; for an rvalue T is X and the parameter is X&& . std::forward then preserves that category. What is perfect forwarding, and why do we need it? Passing arguments through a wrapper to another function without changing their value category or constness, so the callee can still choose copy vs move correctly. Without it you would need an overload per combination of lvalue/rvalue/const for every parameter. Explain SFINAE. If substituting deduced types into a function template's signature yields an invalid type or expression, that candidate is removed from the overload set rather than raising an error. std::enable_if exploits it to enable a function only for suitable types. C++17 if constexpr and C++20 concepts are the clearer modern tools. Full vs partial specialization? Full: all template arguments fixed. Partial: a pattern with some parameters still free ( Foo<T*> ), allowed for class templates and variable templates but not for function templates (use overloading). Why must template definitions be in headers? The compiler needs the body to instantiate it in each translation unit that uses a particular type. If only the declaration is visible, no instantiation happens and the linker cannot find the function. Alternative: explicit instantiation in one .cpp . What is a variadic template and how do you process the pack? A template taking zero or more arguments via a parameter pack ( typename... Args ). Expand with args... . Process by recursion with a base case, or in C++17 with a fold expression like (args + ...) . Templates vs inheritance for polymorphism? Templates give static (compile-time) polymorphism: no runtime cost and inlinable, but the type must be known when compiling and each type duplicates code. Inheritance with virtual functions gives dynamic (runtime) polymorphism: one code path and heterogeneous collections, at the cost of an indirection and heap-allocated objects."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s1","h":"What patterns are and are not","x":"What patterns are and are not A design pattern is a vocabulary : saying \"this is an Observer\" instantly conveys structure and intent to another developer. They come in three families: Family Concerned with Examples Creational how objects are created Singleton, Factory, Builder Structural how objects are composed Adapter, Decorator, Proxy, PIMPL Behavioural how objects interact and share responsibility Strategy, Observer, State, Command, Visitor Patterns are not goals Do not add a pattern for its own sake. Each one adds indirection, so use it only when it solves a real problem you have. A good answer in an interview always includes when not to use it . Modern C++ shortens many of them A lambda or std::function often replaces a whole Strategy or Command class hierarchy. std::visit replaces the Visitor boilerplate. Smart pointers are a Proxy. RAII is a pattern the language supports directly. The ideas matter more than the class diagrams."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s2","h":"Singleton","x":"Singleton Intent: guarantee that a class has exactly one instance and give global access to it (a logger, a configuration store, a hardware manager). class Logger { public: static Logger& instance() { static Logger inst; // created the first time control passes here return inst; // ...and the same object is returned forever after } Logger(const Logger&) = delete; // forbid copies: there must be only one Logger& operator=(const Logger&) = delete; void log(const std::string& msg) { std::cout This is the Meyers Singleton . Step through it: Thread safety: since C++11 the initialization of a function-local static is guaranteed to happen exactly once even if many threads call instance() at the same time (\"magic statics\"). You do not need a mutex or double-checked locking for the creation. Calls on the object itself ( log ) still need their own synchronization if they mutate shared state. Lazy: it is built on first use, which also avoids the static initialization order problem between translation units. Drawbacks (say these in the interview): it is global state in disguise; it hides dependencies (any code can reach it); it makes unit testing hard because you cannot substitute a fake; and destruction happens at program exit in an unspecified order relative to other statics, so using it from another static's destructor can touch a dead object. Alternative: create one object in main and pass it (by reference) to the code that needs it: dependency injection. You still get \"one instance\" without the global."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s3","h":"Factory","x":"Factory Intent: create objects without the caller naming the concrete class. The caller asks for \"a circle\" (by an enum, string or config value) and receives something it can only use through an interface. struct Shape { virtual double area() const = 0; virtual ~Shape() = default; }; struct Circle : Shape { double r; explicit Circle(double r) : r(r) {} double area() const override { return 3.14159 * r * r; } }; struct Square : Shape { double s; explicit Square(double s) : s(s) {} double area() const override { return s * s; } }; enum class Kind { Circle, Square }; std::unique_ptr makeShape(Kind k, double size) { // the factory function switch (k) { case Kind::Circle: return std::make_unique (size); case Kind::Square: return std::make_unique (size); } return nullptr; } auto s = makeShape(Kind::Circle, 2.0); // caller sees only Shape std::cout area(); Return a smart pointer ( unique_ptr ) so ownership is clear and the caller cannot leak it. It converts to shared_ptr if needed. The base class needs a virtual destructor , because the object is deleted through a Shape pointer. Adding a new type means editing the factory. To avoid that (Open/Closed principle), keep a registry : std::map<std::string, std::function<std::unique_ptr<Shape>(double)>> that types register themselves into. Factory Method lets subclasses decide what to create (a virtual create() ); Abstract Factory creates families of related objects (for example a UI toolkit producing a matching Button and Checkbox for each platform)."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s4","h":"Strategy","x":"Strategy Intent: define a family of interchangeable algorithms and let the caller pick one at runtime without changing the code that uses it. Examples: sorting order, pricing rules, compression algorithms, retry policies. // 1. Classic: an interface and several implementations struct PricingStrategy { virtual double apply(double price) const = 0; virtual ~PricingStrategy() = default; }; struct Regular : PricingStrategy { double apply(double p) const override { return p; } }; struct Member : PricingStrategy { double apply(double p) const override { return p * 0.90; } }; class Checkout { std::unique_ptr strategy_; public: explicit Checkout(std::unique_ptr s) : strategy_(std::move(s)) {} double total(double price) const { return strategy_->apply(price); } }; // 2. Modern: the strategy IS a callable. No class hierarchy needed. class Checkout2 { std::function pricing_; public: explicit Checkout2(std::function f) : pricing_(std::move(f)) {} double total(double price) const { return pricing_(price); } }; Checkout2 c([](double p) { return p * 0.70; }); // a lambda is a strategy // 3. Compile-time: the strategy is a template parameter (policy-based design): zero overhead template class Checkout3 { /* ... Pricing{}(price) ... */ }; Try the pricing example: Strategy vs State They look alike (an object delegates to a swappable helper) but differ in intent. With Strategy the client chooses the algorithm and the strategies do not know about each other. With State the object changes its own behaviour as its internal state changes, and the states themselves usually trigger the transitions."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s5","h":"Observer","x":"Observer Intent: when one object (the subject ) changes, automatically notify a list of dependents (the observers ) without the subject knowing who they are. This is the heart of event systems, GUI signals and publish/subscribe. Subscribe and unsubscribe observers below, then change the temperature: class Observer { public: virtual void update(double value) = 0; virtual ~Observer() = default; }; class Subject { std::vector observers_; public: void attach(Observer* o) { observers_.push_back(o); } void detach(Observer* o) { observers_.erase(std::remove(observers_.begin(), observers_.end(), o), observers_.end()); } void set(double v) { value_ = v; auto copy = observers_; // iterate a COPY: an observer may detach itself in update() for (auto* o : copy) o->update(v); } private: double value_ = 0; }; // Modern, lighter version: observers are just callbacks std::vector > listeners; listeners.push_back([](double t) { std::cout Classic Observer bugs Dangling observer: an observer is destroyed but the subject still holds its raw pointer, so the next notification calls a dead object. Fix: observers detach in their destructor (RAII), or the subject stores std::weak_ptr<Observer> and skips the expired ones. Modifying the list while notifying: iterate over a copy, as above. Threads: notifying from another thread needs a lock, and you should not hold it while calling observers (risk of deadlock)."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s6","h":"State","x":"State Intent: let an object change its behaviour when its internal state changes, replacing a tangle of if (state == ...) checks scattered through every method. A turnstile is the classic example. Send events and watch it respond: // 1. Simplest: an enum and a switch. Fine for a few states. enum class State { Locked, Unlocked }; class Turnstile { State s_ = State::Locked; public: void coin() { s_ = State::Unlocked; } void push() { if (s_ == State::Unlocked) s_ = State::Locked; else alarm(); } }; // 2. Classic State pattern: one class per state struct TurnstileState { virtual std::unique_ptr coin() = 0; // each state returns the NEXT state virtual std::unique_ptr push() = 0; virtual ~TurnstileState() = default; }; // 3. Modern: std::variant of state types + std::visit; the compiler checks every state is handled. using StateV = std::variant ; Use a state machine whenever the valid operations depend on \"what mode am I in\": connection handling (connecting, connected, closing), parsers, protocol handlers and device controllers. Making transitions explicit also makes illegal states unrepresentable and easy to test."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s7","h":"Other patterns worth recognising","x":"Other patterns worth recognising Pattern Idea Where you already see it in C++ RAII object owns a resource and releases it in its destructor vector , unique_ptr , lock_guard Adapter wrap an interface so it matches the one clients expect std::stack (adapts deque ), wrapping a C library Decorator wrap an object to add behaviour while keeping its interface buffered/compressed stream wrappers, logging wrappers Proxy a stand-in that controls access to another object shared_ptr , vector<bool>::reference Command turn a request into an object (queue it, undo it) task queues of std::function , thread pools Iterator traverse a collection without exposing its structure the entire STL Visitor add operations to a type hierarchy without changing it std::visit with std::variant Builder construct a complex object step by step fluent config objects: Req().url(u).timeout(5).build() PIMPL hide implementation behind a pointer to keep headers stable and compile times low library public headers CRTP a class derives from a template instantiated with itself: static polymorphism mix-ins, std::enable_shared_from_this // CRTP: compile-time polymorphism, no vtable template struct Shape { double area() const { return static_cast (this)->areaImpl(); } }; struct Circle : Shape { double r = 1; double areaImpl() const { return 3.14159 * r * r; } };"},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s8","h":"SOLID principles","x":"SOLID principles Patterns are tools; SOLID explains why they work. Interviewers love it as a follow-up. Principle In one sentence Pattern that serves it S Single Responsibility a class has one reason to change split parsing, logic and I/O into separate classes O Open/Closed open for extension, closed for modification: add behaviour by adding code, not editing old code Strategy, Factory registry, Decorator L Liskov Substitution a derived object must be usable wherever the base is expected without breaking correctness correct inheritance hierarchies I Interface Segregation prefer several small interfaces over one fat one narrow abstract classes D Dependency Inversion depend on abstractions, not concrete classes; inject dependencies Strategy, Observer, dependency injection (instead of Singleton) More in your notes: Module 4: Design Patterns with runnable examples for Singleton, State, Strategy and Observer."},{"f":"06-design-patterns.html","n":"Design Patterns in C++","id":"s9","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet How do you make a Singleton thread-safe in C++? Use a function-local static (Meyers Singleton). Since C++11 its initialization is guaranteed thread-safe and happens exactly once. Alternatives are std::call_once with an std::once_flag , or creating it before threads start. Hand-written double-checked locking is easy to get wrong. Remember the object's own methods still need locking if they mutate shared state. What is wrong with Singleton? It is global mutable state with a nice name: hidden dependencies, tight coupling, hard to test or mock, awkward lifetime and destruction order, and a problem for multithreading. Prefer passing a reference (dependency injection) unless a single shared instance is truly inherent. Strategy vs State? Both delegate behaviour to a swappable object. Strategy: the client selects an interchangeable algorithm and strategies are independent. State: the context changes its behaviour as its own state changes, and transitions are driven by the states or context. What problems can Observer cause? Dangling observers (destroyed but still registered), modification of the observer list during notification, unexpected notification order, cascading updates and performance issues, and thread-safety issues. Fixes: RAII detach, weak_ptr , iterate over a copy, and do not hold locks while calling out. Why return unique_ptr from a factory? It expresses that the caller takes sole ownership, prevents leaks, and the caller can convert it to a shared_ptr when sharing is needed. Returning a raw owning pointer is ambiguous and error-prone. How do you replace a virtual-function Strategy in modern C++? With a callable: store a std::function (runtime flexibility) or take a template parameter (zero overhead, compile-time choice). A lambda becomes the strategy, and no class hierarchy is needed. What is the Open/Closed principle? Software entities should be open to extension but closed to modification: you add new behaviour (a new strategy, a new shape type registered in a factory) without editing the existing, tested code."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s1","h":"Why threads? The mental model","x":"Why threads? The mental model A normal program has one thread of execution : one cook working through a recipe, one instruction at a time. A thread is an independent flow of execution. A program can have many, so several things can progress at once, like several cooks in one kitchen. Term Meaning Process a running program with its own private memory. Processes are isolated from each other. Thread a flow of execution inside a process. Threads of one process share the same memory (heap and globals) but each has its own stack and registers. Concurrency several tasks in progress at overlapping times (can be interleaved on a single core). Parallelism several tasks running at literally the same instant on different cores. Why use threads? (1) Speed : split heavy work across cores. (2) Responsiveness : keep a UI or a network handler alive while a slow job runs. (3) Waiting : do other work while one thread blocks on I/O. The whole difficulty in one sentence Threads share memory, and the operating system may switch between them at any moment , between any two instructions. Everything in this lesson is about keeping shared memory correct despite that. std::thread::hardware_concurrency(); // how many hardware threads (cores x hyperthreads) this machine has, e.g. 8"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s2","h":"Your first thread","x":"Your first thread #include #include void hello(int id) { std::cout Two lines are printed by two threads that run at the same time, so the order is not fixed . Run it repeatedly and you may see either: main continues worker 1 running worker 1 running or main continues That non-determinism is the defining feature of threaded code. A test that passed once proves almost nothing. The rules you must know Always join() or detach() before the std::thread object is destroyed. If a thread is still joinable when its object dies, the program calls std::terminate . join() waits for it. detach() lets it run on its own; then you can no longer wait for it, and it must not touch anything that might be destroyed. Arguments are copied into the thread. To share a variable by reference you must say so with std::ref . Threads are move-only : std::thread b = std::move(a); . An exception that escapes a thread's function calls std::terminate . Catch inside the thread (or pass it out with a future , see later). void addOne(int& n) { ++n; } int x = 0; std::thread t1(addOne, std::ref(x)); // std::ref: pass x by reference (without it this will not even compile) t1.join(); // x is now 1 std::thread t2([] { std::cout The classic detach bug void f() { int local = 0; std::thread t([&] { ++local; }); // captures local BY REFERENCE t.detach(); } // f returns, local is destroyed, the thread still writes to it Capture by value (or join() ) whenever the thread could outlive the variable. C++20: std::jthread It joins automatically in its destructor (and supports cooperative cancellation with std::stop_token ), so you cannot forget the join ."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s3","h":"Shared data and data races","x":"Shared data and data races Two threads each add 1 to a shared counter. What could go wrong? int counter = 0; void work() { for (int i = 0; i The statement ++counter looks like one step but the CPU performs three : load the value from memory into a register: r = counter add one to the register: r = r + 1 store the register back to memory: counter = r If thread B's load happens between thread A's load and store, both start from the same old value, both write the same new value, and one increment is lost . Try to make it happen. Click Step A and Step B in any order you like (you are playing the operating system's scheduler): Hint: let A do r = counter , then let B run completely, then let A finish. Or just press Run 500 random schedules and see how often the \"impossible\" results appear. A realistic caveat The simulator may switch threads between every pair of instructions, far more often than a real operating system does, so it makes the bug look very common. On a real machine the same race might fail once in thousands of runs, which is exactly why these bugs survive testing and bite in production. Data race = undefined behavior A data race is two threads accessing the same memory at the same time, with at least one writing and no synchronization. In C++ this is undefined behavior , not merely \"a slightly wrong number\": the compiler and CPU may reorder things in ways that make the program do anything. A race condition is the broader logic bug where the result depends on timing. Three ways to be safe Strategy How 1. Do not share each thread works on its own data (local variables, thread_local ), and you combine results afterwards. The best option when possible. 2. Share, but never modify read-only data ( const ) is safe to read from many threads. 3. Share and protect use a mutex (next section) or an atomic so only one thread changes the data at a time."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s4","h":"Mutexes: taking turns","x":"Mutexes: taking turns A mutex (mutual exclusion lock) lets only one thread at a time execute a protected region, the critical section . A thread calls lock() ; if another thread holds the lock, it waits . When the owner calls unlock() , a waiter may proceed. Here are the same two threads with the increment wrapped in a lock (look at the mutex state box, and at how a thread shows \"waiting for m\"): Run 500 random schedules: the result is now always 4 , whatever order the scheduler picks. std::mutex m; int counter = 0; void work() { for (int i = 0; i lock(m); // locks now, unlocks automatically at the end of the block ++counter; } } Always use RAII locks, never raw lock()/unlock() If code between a manual lock() and unlock() throws or returns early, the mutex stays locked forever and every other thread freezes. The RAII wrappers unlock in their destructor no matter how the scope is left: Type Use it when std::lock_guard<std::mutex> the simple default: lock for the whole scope std::unique_lock<std::mutex> you need to unlock early, relock, defer locking, or use a condition variable (required for cv.wait ) std::scoped_lock (C++17) locking several mutexes at once without deadlock std::shared_mutex + shared_lock many readers or one writer, when reads vastly outnumber writes Protecting a class properly class SafeCounter { mutable std::mutex m_; // mutable: so const getters can still lock it int value_ = 0; public: void increment() { std::lock_guard lk(m_); ++value_; } int get() const { std::lock_guard lk(m_); return value_; } }; Rules for good locking Keep critical sections short : lock, touch the shared data, unlock. Do the slow work (I/O, computation) outside the lock. Put the mutex next to the data it protects , inside the same class, and never hand out references to that data. Never call unknown code (callbacks, virtual functions) while holding a lock: it may try to take the same lock, or another one. Locking costs time. If every access is locked, threads queue up and you get contention : your parallel program becomes serial."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s5","h":"Deadlock","x":"Deadlock A deadlock is when threads wait for each other forever. The classic recipe: thread A holds lock a and wants b , while thread B holds b and wants a . Neither can proceed. Here the two threads take the locks in opposite order . Try to deadlock them by hand: step A once (takes a ), step B once (takes b ), then try to go on. Press \"Run 500 random schedules\": a large share of runs end in a deadlock. In real programs it is worse: it may happen once in a million runs, always in production, and never under the debugger. A deadlock needs all four of these (the Coffman conditions), so removing any one prevents it: Mutual exclusion : the resource can be held by only one thread. Hold and wait : a thread holds one lock while waiting for another. No preemption : a lock cannot be taken away from its holder. Circular wait : A waits for B, B waits for A (a cycle). The fix: one global lock order If every thread takes a before b , a cycle is impossible. Here are the same threads with a consistent order: std::mutex a, b; void thread1() { std::scoped_lock lk(a, b); /* ... */ } // C++17: locks both with a deadlock-avoidance algorithm void thread2() { std::scoped_lock lk(b, a); /* ... */ } // OK even though the order differs: scoped_lock sorts it out // pre-C++17: std::lock(a, b); // lock both atomically (deadlock-free) std::lock_guard la(a, std::adopt_lock); // adopt the already-held locks so RAII still unlocks std::lock_guard lb(b, std::adopt_lock); Interview angle \"How do you prevent deadlock?\" Impose a global lock ordering; lock multiple mutexes with std::scoped_lock ; avoid holding a lock while calling out to other code; hold locks briefly; avoid nested locking when you can; or use try_lock with back-off. Also: locking a non-recursive mutex you already own is undefined behavior (in practice, a thread that deadlocks with itself)."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s6","h":"Atomics","x":"Atomics For a single simple variable, a mutex is overkill. std::atomic<T> makes operations on one variable indivisible : no other thread can see a half-finished update. It is implemented with special CPU instructions and usually does not block. Compare with the broken counter: the increment is now one single step. #include std::atomic counter{0}; counter++; // one indivisible read-modify-write counter.fetch_add(1); // same thing, explicit int v = counter.load(); // atomic read counter.store(5); // atomic write std::atomic stop{false}; // a very common use: a shutdown flag void worker() { while (!stop.load()) { /* work */ } } // main: stop.store(true); t.join(); // compare_exchange: \"set it to desired ONLY if it still equals expected\" int expected = counter.load(); while (!counter.compare_exchange_weak(expected, expected * 2)) { // someone changed it: `expected` was refreshed to the current value, so just retry } Use an atomic for Do not use it for a counter, a flag, a pointer you swap, statistics an invariant that spans several variables (for example \"balance and history must change together\"). For that you need a mutex. Memory ordering (what you need to know) Compilers and CPUs reorder memory operations for speed. A plain int written by one thread has no guarantee of ever being visible to another. Atomics take an optional memory order to say how much ordering you need: Order Meaning Use memory_order_seq_cst (default) strongest: all threads agree on one global order of atomic operations when in doubt. Simple and correct. memory_order_release / acquire a release store makes everything the thread wrote before it visible to a thread that does an acquire load and sees that value publishing data with a flag memory_order_relaxed atomicity only, no ordering plain counters and statistics int data = 0; std::atomic ready{false}; // producer thread // consumer thread data = 42; while (!ready.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) { } ready.store(true, std::memory_order_release); std::cout volatile is NOT thread-safe In C++, volatile only stops the compiler optimising an access away (useful for hardware registers). It gives no atomicity and no ordering between threads. Use std::atomic . Interview angle Know: atomic vs mutex (lock-free single variable vs general critical section); is_lock_free() ; that default seq_cst is the safe choice and weaker orders are an optimisation needing proof; and the ABA problem (a CAS succeeds because a value changed A to B and back to A, hiding that something happened; fixed with version counters or hazard pointers)."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s7","h":"Condition variables","x":"Condition variables Sometimes a thread must wait until something happens : a consumer waits for the producer to put an item in a queue. Looping on while (queue.empty()) {} burns 100% CPU (\"busy waiting\"). A condition variable lets the thread sleep until another thread wakes it. It always works together with a mutex and the shared state it guards. std::mutex m; std::condition_variable cv; std::queue q; // CONSUMER std::unique_lock lk(m); cv.wait(lk, [] { return !q.empty(); }); // sleep until the predicate is true int x = q.front(); q.pop(); // PRODUCER { std::lock_guard lk(m); q.push(42); } cv.notify_one(); // wake one waiting thread Watch the hand-over between the two threads: Three rules that interviewers check Always wait with a predicate ( cv.wait(lk, pred) ), which is the same as while (!pred()) cv.wait(lk); . The OS can wake a thread for no reason ( spurious wakeup ), and another consumer might have taken the item first, so the condition must be re-checked. Change the shared state while holding the mutex , then notify. If you modify it without the lock, a waiter can check the condition, see it false, and be about to sleep just as you notify: the notification is lost and it sleeps forever ( lost wakeup ). The waiter must hold a std::unique_lock (not lock_guard ) because wait must unlock the mutex while sleeping and relock it on wake-up. notify_one() wakes a single waiter; notify_all() wakes every waiter (use it when the condition may allow several to proceed, or for shutdown). A reusable thread-safe queue template class SafeQueue { std::queue q_; std::mutex m_; std::condition_variable cv_; public: void push(T v) { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); q_.push(std::move(v)); } cv_.notify_one(); // notify AFTER releasing the lock: the woken thread can take it at once } T pop() { // blocks until an item exists std::unique_lock lk(m_); cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return !q_.empty(); }); T v = std::move(q_.front()); q_.pop(); return v; } };"},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s8","h":"Futures and async","x":"Futures and async Often you do not want to manage a thread by hand: you just want to run a function in the background and get its result later . A std::future<T> is a placeholder for a value that will be ready in the future. #include int slowSquare(int n) { std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::seconds(1)); return n * n; } std::future f = std::async(std::launch::async, slowSquare, 7); // starts on another thread doOtherWork(); // main keeps working meanwhile int result = f.get(); // wait if needed, then get 49 f.get() blocks until the result is ready, and it can be called only once. f.wait() waits without taking the value; f.wait_for(...) waits with a timeout. Exceptions travel with the result : if the task throws, f.get() rethrows it in the calling thread. This is the clean way to report errors from threads. Pass std::launch::async explicitly. With the default policy the implementation is allowed to defer the call and run it lazily (on the calling thread) only when you call get() . A future obtained from std::async blocks in its destructor until the task finishes. So std::async(...) with the returned future discarded runs the task effectively synchronously. // std::promise / std::future: a one-shot channel between two threads you manage yourself std::promise p; std::future f = p.get_future(); std::thread t([&p] { p.set_value(42); }); // the worker fulfils the promise (or p.set_exception(...)) std::cout std::packaged_task wraps a function so that its result goes into a future, which is how thread pools return results from queued tasks."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s9","h":"Thread pools and producer/consumer","x":"Thread pools and producer/consumer Creating a thread per task is expensive (tens of microseconds, plus memory for its stack) and creating thousands overloads the scheduler. A thread pool starts a fixed number of worker threads once. Tasks are put in a queue; each idle worker takes the next one. This is the producer/consumer pattern: producers submit work, consumers (workers) process it. class ThreadPool { std::vector workers_; std::queue > tasks_; std::mutex m_; std::condition_variable cv_; bool stop_ = false; public: explicit ThreadPool(std::size_t n) { for (std::size_t i = 0; i task; { std::unique_lock lk(m_); cv_.wait(lk, [this] { return stop_ || !tasks_.empty(); }); if (stop_ && tasks_.empty()) return; // finish remaining tasks, then exit task = std::move(tasks_.front()); tasks_.pop(); } // lock released BEFORE running the task task(); } }); } void submit(std::function f) { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); tasks_.push(std::move(f)); } cv_.notify_one(); } ~ThreadPool() { { std::lock_guard lk(m_); stop_ = true; } // request shutdown under the lock cv_.notify_all(); // wake every sleeping worker for (auto& w : workers_) w.join(); // wait for them to finish } }; ThreadPool pool(std::thread::hardware_concurrency()); pool.submit([] { std::cout Read it once slowly, because it combines everything: a mutex protecting the queue, a condition variable to sleep and wake, a stop flag for graceful shutdown, and RAII in the destructor. Notice that the task runs outside the lock, otherwise only one task could ever run at a time. Sizing: for CPU-bound work use about hardware_concurrency() workers (more just fight over cores: oversubscription ). For work that mostly waits on I/O you can use more."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s10","h":"Performance and real-time concerns","x":"Performance and real-time concerns Correct is step one. Fast and predictable is step two, and it is what matters for low-latency systems such as market-data feed handlers. Problem What happens Remedy Lock contention many threads queue on one mutex, so the program is effectively serial (Amdahl's law: the serial part limits speed-up) shorter critical sections, split data into independent shards each with its own lock, per-thread data merged at the end False sharing two threads write different variables that sit on the same cache line (typically 64 bytes), so the line ping-pongs between cores and everything slows down pad or align hot per-thread data: alignas(64) std::atomic<int> c; Allocation on the hot path new / malloc can take a lock inside and has unpredictable latency pre-allocate ( reserve ), object pools, ring buffers Priority inversion a low-priority thread holds a lock a high-priority thread needs, and a medium-priority thread keeps the low one from running priority-inheritance mutexes, shorter locks, lock-free handoff Context switches blocking and waking threads costs microseconds busy-wait briefly (spin) on a core you own, pin threads to cores, avoid sleeping on the hot path Lock-free: a single-producer single-consumer ring buffer When exactly one thread produces and one consumes, you can pass data with no lock at all using two atomic indices. Each index is written by only one thread, so no conflict is possible; release/acquire makes the data visible. This pattern is a staple of low-latency code. template class SpscQueue { T buf_[N]; std::atomic head_{0}; // next slot to read: written ONLY by the consumer std::atomic tail_{0}; // next slot to write: written ONLY by the producer public: bool push(const T& v) { // producer thread only auto t = tail_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); auto next = (t + 1) % N; if (next == head_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // full buf_[t] = v; // write the data... tail_.store(next, std::memory_order_release); // ...then publish it return true; } bool pop(T& out) { // consumer thread only auto h = head_.load(std::memory_order_relaxed); if (h == tail_.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // empty out = buf_[h]; head_.store((h + 1) % N, std::memory_order_release); // free the slot return true; } }; Be honest about lock-free in interviews Lock-free code is hard to write and verify. Say you would start with a mutex-based queue, measure, and move to a lock-free structure only if profiling shows the lock is the bottleneck. The SPSC queue is the one case simple enough to write confidently."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s11","h":"Debugging and common bugs","x":"Debugging and common bugs Threading bugs are timing-dependent: they vanish when you add a printf or run under a debugger (a \"Heisenbug\"). Use tools built to catch them: ThreadSanitizer : compile and link with -fsanitize=thread -g (GCC or Clang). It detects data races at runtime and prints both conflicting accesses with stack traces. Run your tests with it routinely. Helgrind / DRD (Valgrind): finds races and lock-order violations (potential deadlocks). Logging with thread ids and timestamps, a core dump / gdb thread apply all bt to see where every thread is stuck in a hang. Symptom Likely cause Wrong totals, occasional crashes, \"works on my machine\" data race (missing lock or atomic) Program hangs, CPU idle deadlock (or a lost wakeup / a thread waiting for a notification that already happened) Program hangs, CPU 100% busy-wait loop, or a livelock (threads keep reacting to each other without progress) Crash at exit, or on a detached thread detached thread using destroyed data; thread still joinable at destruction ( std::terminate ) Slower with more threads lock contention, false sharing, oversubscription Garbled console output several threads writing to std::cout : lines interleave. Build the whole line first and print once, or guard with a mutex. Handy vocabulary Thread-safe : can be used from several threads at once without external locking (for example, the atomic reference count of shared_ptr ; not the object it points to). thread_local : each thread gets its own copy of the variable. No sharing, no races. Function-local static initialization is thread-safe since C++11 (the basis of the Meyers Singleton). std::call_once(flag, fn) : run initialization code exactly once across all threads. Further reading: your notes in Module 3: multithreading and synchronization (with runnable examples ), the modern-cpp-tutorial concurrency chapter (threads, mutexes, futures, condition variables, atomics and the memory model), and the modern-cpp-features notes on std::thread , std::async , std::scoped_lock and parallel algorithms."},{"f":"07-multithreading.html","n":"Multithreading in C++","id":"s12","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Say each answer out loud, then open it to compare. What is the difference between a process and a thread? A process has its own address space; threads of one process share memory but each has its own stack and registers. Thread creation and switching are cheaper, communication is easy (shared memory) but needs synchronization. What is a data race? Is it the same as a race condition? A data race is unsynchronised concurrent access to the same memory with at least one write: undefined behavior in C++. A race condition is the wider class of bugs where correctness depends on timing, which can exist even without a data race (two correctly locked steps that needed to be one atomic operation). What happens if a std::thread is destroyed while still joinable? std::terminate is called. Always join() or detach() ; std::jthread (C++20) joins automatically. lock_guard vs unique_lock vs scoped_lock ? lock_guard : simplest RAII lock for a scope. unique_lock : flexible (unlock/relock, deferred, movable) and needed by condition variables. scoped_lock (C++17): locks multiple mutexes at once with deadlock avoidance. What is a deadlock and how do you prevent it? Threads wait for each other's locks forever. Needs mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption and circular wait. Prevent with a consistent global lock order, std::scoped_lock / std::lock for multiple mutexes, short critical sections, no calls to foreign code under a lock, or try_lock . Why does condition_variable::wait need a predicate / a loop? Spurious wakeups (a thread may wake without a notification) and because the state may change again between the notification and the thread reacquiring the lock. The predicate re-checks the real condition. Why must the shared condition be modified under the mutex? Otherwise the waiter could check the condition (false), then the notifier changes it and notifies, then the waiter goes to sleep: a lost wakeup. The mutex makes check-and-sleep atomic with respect to the update. std::atomic vs mutex? An atomic makes operations on a single variable indivisible, usually lock-free and cheap. A mutex protects any critical section, including invariants over several variables, but may block. Use atomics for counters and flags, mutexes for compound state. Is volatile a substitute for atomic ? No. volatile prevents some compiler optimisations but gives no atomicity and no inter-thread ordering. Using it for synchronization is a data race. What are the memory orders? seq_cst (default, strongest, one global order), acquire / release (pair a release store with an acquire load to publish data), relaxed (atomicity only). Start with the default; weaken only with a reason and a proof. std::async vs std::thread ? async returns a future carrying the result and any exception, and may use a pool or defer execution (use launch::async to force a thread). thread gives raw control with no result channel. A future from async blocks in its destructor. What is false sharing? Threads writing different variables on the same cache line force the line to bounce between cores, slowing everything. Fix by padding or aligning per-thread data to the cache line size ( alignas(64) ). Why avoid locks and heap allocation on a real-time hot path? Both can block or take unpredictable time (contention, priority inversion, allocator locks). Use preallocated buffers and pools, per-thread data and lock-free structures such as an SPSC ring buffer to keep latency bounded. Is a function-local static initialised thread-safely? Yes, since C++11 initialization happens exactly once even with concurrent callers (\"magic statics\"). That is why the Meyers Singleton is thread-safe. Using the object afterwards still needs its own synchronization. How would you find a data race in a large program? Build and run the tests with ThreadSanitizer ( -fsanitize=thread ), and Helgrind for lock-order problems; review shared state and make sure every access has a lock or is atomic; reduce sharing by design."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s1","h":"std::string and std::string_view","x":"std::string and std::string_view std::string owns its characters: it allocates, copies and frees them. std::string_view (C++17) is a non-owning pointer plus a length. Passing a view costs two words and never allocates, but the view is only valid while the text it points at is alive. #include #include void log(std::string_view msg); // accepts std::string, string literal, char array... without copying std::string s = \"hello world\"; log(s); log(\"literal\"); // no temporary std::string is built auto pos = s.find(\"world\"); // 6 if (pos != std::string::npos) { // find returns npos (a huge value) when nothing matches std::string_view word = std::string_view(s).substr(pos); // \"world\", no copy } Classic bug: dangling view std::string_view bad = std::string(\"temporary\"); // the string dies at the semicolon // 'bad' now points at freed memory: undefined behaviour if you read it std::string_view f() { std::string local = \"oops\"; return local; // returns a view of a dead local } A view must never outlive the string it looks at, and it is not guaranteed to be null-terminated , so do not hand sv.data() to a C API that expects a C string. Small string optimisation (SSO): short strings (around 15 characters on common implementations) live inside the std::string object itself, with no heap allocation. Use std::string_view for read-only function parameters . Keep std::string for anything you store. s += a + b style concatenation can create temporaries; use append or reserve when building large strings in a loop. Interview angle \" const std::string& or std::string_view for a parameter?\" View when you only read, because it accepts literals without constructing a temporary string. Reference when you need a real std::string (for example to call a function that needs null termination)."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s2","h":"Regular expressions: the basics","x":"Regular expressions: the basics A regular expression describes a set of strings . <regex> gives you three entry points, and the difference between the first two is a favourite question: Function Question it answers std::regex_match Does the whole string match the pattern? std::regex_search Does some part of the string match? (first match; captures in std::smatch ) std::regex_replace Replace every match with a replacement string. #include std::regex re(\"ab+c\"); std::regex_match(\"abbbc\", re); // true : the entire string fits std::regex_match(\"xabbbcx\", re); // false : extra characters around it std::regex_search(\"xabbbcx\", re); // true : found inside Syntax Meaning Example . any character a.b matches aab , axb ? * + 0 or 1, 0 or more, 1 or more ab*c matches ac , abbbc {n} {n,m} exactly n, between n and m ab{3}c matches abbbc [abc] [a-z] [^a-z] one of, a range, none of a[bcd]e (x|y) group and alternation gr(e|a)y matches grey , gray ^ $ start and end of the string ^\\d+$ \\d \\w \\s digit, word character, whitespace \\w+@\\w+ Try patterns yourself. std::regex uses the ECMAScript grammar by default, which is the same grammar this tester (the browser's own regex engine) uses for everything in the table above. Backslashes in C++ strings In a normal string literal you must write \"\\\\d+\" . A raw string literal avoids the doubling: std::regex re(R\"(\\d+)\"); . POSIX classes such as [[:alnum:]] work in std::regex but not in the browser tester."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s3","h":"Captures, replace and iterating matches","x":"Captures, replace and iterating matches Parentheses make capture groups . After a successful search, std::smatch holds the whole match at index 0 and each group after it. std::string line = \"temp=21.5C humidity=40%\"; std::regex kv(R\"((\\w+)=([\\d.]+))\"); std::smatch m; if (std::regex_search(line, m, kv)) { // m[0] = \"temp=21.5\" m[1] = \"temp\" m[2] = \"21.5\" std::cout \" 21.5, then humidity -> 40 // replace: $1, $2 refer to groups std::string swapped = std::regex_replace(line, kv, \"$2:$1\"); // \"21.5:tempC 40:humidity%\" (only the matched parts change) Practical limits of std::regex Constructing a regex is expensive (it compiles the pattern). Build it once, not inside a loop. std::regex is generally slow compared with hand-written parsing or libraries such as RE2 or CTRE. In hot paths or latency-sensitive embedded code, prefer simple find / from_chars parsing. Patterns with nested quantifiers like (a+)+b can backtrack exponentially on bad input. Never feed untrusted input to a pattern you have not reasoned about. An invalid pattern throws std::regex_error at construction. Interview angle \"Why does regex_match(\"hello world\", std::regex(\"hello\")) return false?\" Because regex_match needs the pattern to cover the entire string. regex_search would return true."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s4","h":"Time with std::chrono","x":"Time with std::chrono <chrono> is built from three ideas that are worth saying in this order in an interview: Duration : an amount of time, a count plus a unit ( seconds , milliseconds ...). It is type-safe, so you cannot confuse seconds with milliseconds. Time point : an instant, measured as a duration since a clock's epoch. Clock : the source of time points. Clock Behaviour Use it for system_clock Wall-clock time. Can jump (NTP, manual changes, daylight handling by the OS). Convertible to time_t . Timestamps, dates shown to people steady_clock Monotonic : never goes backwards. Measuring elapsed time, timeouts high_resolution_clock Smallest tick the implementation has. Often just an alias of one of the above. Rarely the right choice; prefer steady_clock #include #include using namespace std::chrono_literals; auto t0 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); std::this_thread::sleep_for(250ms); // duration literal auto t1 = std::chrono::steady_clock::now(); auto elapsed = std::chrono::duration_cast (t1 - t0); std::cout (s); // 1 (truncates) Use steady_clock for intervals Measuring elapsed time with system_clock can give negative or wildly wrong results if the system clock is adjusted while you measure. This matters for timeouts in real-time and embedded code."},{"f":"08-strings-regex-time.html","n":"Strings, Regex and Time","id":"s5","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet Say each answer out loud, then open it. When is a string_view dangerous? When it outlives the string it views: views of temporaries, of locals returned from a function, or of a string that is later modified or reallocated. regex_match versus regex_search ? regex_match must match the whole input. regex_search finds a match anywhere inside it. How do you iterate over every match? std::sregex_iterator (or std::cregex_iterator for C strings) over the range, compared with a default-constructed end iterator. Which clock for a timeout, and why? steady_clock , because it is monotonic and unaffected by changes to the system time. Why can seconds = milliseconds not compile but milliseconds = seconds can? Converting to a coarser unit would truncate, so it requires an explicit duration_cast . Converting to a finer unit is lossless, so it is implicit. Output: what does this print? std::string s = \"abc\"; std::cout << s.find('z') == std::string::npos; It does not compile as intended: << binds tighter than == , so it tries to compare the stream with npos . Parenthesise: std::cout << (s.find('z') == std::string::npos); prints 1."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s1","h":"Type traits","x":"Type traits A type trait is a template in <type_traits> that answers a question about a type at compile time, or transforms one type into another. Query traits expose a ::value (and, since C++17, a shorter _v variable template). Transformation traits expose a ::type (and, since C++14, a shorter _t alias). #include static_assert(std::is_integral_v ); // query: true static_assert(!std::is_integral_v ); static_assert(std::is_same_v >); // transform, then compare template T multiply(T a, T b) { static_assert(std::is_arithmetic_v , \"multiply needs a number type\"); return a * b; } // multiply(std::string(\"a\"), std::string(\"b\")); // compile error with YOUR message Explore what the common traits say about different types. Look closely at the references and const rows: they are where people guess wrong. Interview angle \"What is the difference between static_assert and assert ?\" static_assert is checked at compile time on constant expressions and costs nothing at run time. assert is a run-time check that disappears when NDEBUG is defined."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s2","h":"enable_if and SFINAE","x":"enable_if and SFINAE When the compiler tries every overload of a template and substitution produces an invalid type, it does not report an error: it just drops that candidate. This rule is SFINAE (Substitution Failure Is Not An Error). std::enable_if uses it to switch overloads on or off by a condition. template , bool> = true> void print(const T& v) { std::cout , bool> = true> void print(const T& v) { std::cout It works, but the syntax is noisy and the error messages are poor. Modern C++ gives you two better tools: Tool Standard Looks like if constexpr C++17 Branch inside one function; the untaken branch is not even compiled for that type Concepts / requires C++20 template <std::integral T> void print(T v); template void print(const T& v) { if constexpr (std::is_integral_v ) std::cout // C++20: constrained template, readable errors T twice(T v) { return v * 2; } Plain if is not enough With an ordinary if (std::is_integral_v<T>) both branches must compile for every T , so a branch that only makes sense for integers breaks the build for double . if constexpr discards the false branch inside a template."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s3","h":"constexpr , consteval , constinit","x":"constexpr , consteval , constinit Instead of tricks with templates, you can usually just write an ordinary function and ask for compile-time evaluation. Keyword Meaning constexpr variable Must be initialised with a constant expression; usable where constants are required (array sizes, template arguments). constexpr function Can run at compile time if its arguments are constants; can still be called at run time with ordinary values. consteval (C++20) An \"immediate\" function: every call must be evaluated at compile time. constinit (C++20) The variable is initialised at compile time (no static-initialisation-order problems), but remains mutable. constexpr int factorial(int n) { return n constexpr does not force compile time A constexpr function called with a non-constant argument simply runs at run time. To guarantee compile-time evaluation, assign the result to a constexpr variable (or use consteval )."},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s4","h":"Template metaprogramming (TMP)","x":"Template metaprogramming (TMP) Before constexpr functions could do loops and recursion, compile-time computation was done by recursive template instantiation . You will meet this style in older code and in interview questions. template struct Fact { static constexpr int value = Fact ::value * N; // the compiler instantiates Fact , Fact , ... }; template <> struct Fact { // full specialisation ends the recursion static constexpr int value = 1; }; static_assert(Fact ::value == 120); Each Fact<N> is a separate class created by the compiler. The result is a constant baked into the program: zero run-time cost. Limits: compilers cap template recursion depth (often a few hundred), and deep instantiation slows compilation and bloats error messages. Today: prefer a constexpr function for values, and traits plus if constexpr /concepts for types. Write TMP only when you must compute types . Task Old style Modern style Compute a value recursive struct with ::value constexpr function Pick code by type enable_if overloads if constexpr or concepts Reject bad types obscure substitution errors static_assert / requires"},{"f":"09-compile-time.html","n":"Type Traits and Compile-time C++","id":"s5","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet What does SFINAE stand for and why does it exist? Substitution Failure Is Not An Error: an invalid substitution while deducing a template removes that candidate from overload resolution instead of causing a hard error. It is what makes enable_if overload selection work. std::is_same_v<int, const int> ? False. They are different types. const and references are part of the type, which is why traits like std::decay_t and std::remove_cvref_t exist. constexpr function versus consteval function? constexpr may run at compile time or run time. consteval must run at compile time and is a compile error otherwise. Why is if constexpr better than if in a template? The discarded branch is not instantiated, so code that is invalid for some T does not break the build for that T . How do you avoid writing the factorial struct by hand today? Write constexpr int factorial(int n) and use it in a constexpr context. It is shorter, readable and can also run at run time."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s1","h":"Why coding rules exist","x":"Why coding rules exist C++ lets you write code that compiles but has undefined , unspecified or implementation-defined behaviour, or is simply easy to misread. In a car or medical device, a rare misbehaviour is not acceptable, so projects agree on rules that remove the risky corners. Coding rules come in two kinds: Kind Purpose Examples Guidelines (language usage) Avoid errors from using language features in vulnerable or unclear ways initialise every variable; no C-style casts; no unchecked implicit narrowing Style (form) Make code uniform and tidy so reviews are about logic indentation, brace position, naming of classes, functions and members Everyone on the project must know the rules and follow them, and rules are only useful if they are checked automatically ."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s2","h":"MISRA and AUTOSAR C++14","x":"MISRA and AUTOSAR C++14 MISRA C / MISRA C++ are among the best-known rule sets. They began in the automotive industry and are now used widely in safety-critical embedded work. MISRA C++:2008 targets C++03. AUTOSAR C++14 is a guideline for using C++14 in safety-related and critical environments. It is based on MISRA C++:2008 and other leading standards, updated for modern C++ (smart pointers, move semantics, constexpr and so on). Both classify rules by strictness (for example required versus advisory) and let a project record a justified deviation when a rule must be broken. Standards keep evolving (MISRA C++:2023 was written to supersede both MISRA C++:2008 and AUTOSAR C++14), so always check which version your project mandates. Say this in an interview \"I follow the project's coding standard, such as AUTOSAR C++14, check it with a static analyser in CI, and document any deviation with a reason rather than silently working around the tool.\""},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s3","h":"Typical rules, and why","x":"Typical rules, and why The exact rule numbers differ between standards, so learn the themes . Each one below is a pattern that is legal C++ but risky. Try to name the problem before you open each answer. int count; if (ready) count = compute(); use(count); // what if ready was false? What is wrong? Reading an uninitialised local is undefined behaviour. Rule theme: initialise every object before use ( int count{}; ). double d = 3.9; int i = (int)d; // silently truncates Base* b = (Base*)someObject; // could be reinterpret, static or const cast: you cannot tell What is wrong? A C-style cast hides which conversion happens and can do dangerous ones silently. Rule theme: use the named casts ( static_cast and so on) so the intent is visible and greppable, and avoid reinterpret_cast and const_cast . Widget* w = new Widget; process(w); // if this throws, w leaks delete w; What is wrong? Manual ownership leaks on early returns and exceptions. Rule theme: manage resources with RAII : std::make_unique<Widget>() , containers, no naked new / delete . Many embedded standards go further and forbid dynamic allocation after start-up, to avoid fragmentation and unpredictable timing. uint8_t level = 300; // wraps silently to 44 int total = a * b; // int overflow is undefined behaviour What is wrong? Values change silently or overflow. Rule themes: no implicit narrowing (use brace-initialisation uint8_t level{300}; which the compiler rejects), use fixed-width types, and check arithmetic that can overflow. int sum(const Node* n) { return n ? n->v + sum(n->next) : 0; } What is wrong? Recursion depth depends on the data, so stack usage cannot be bounded and may overflow. Embedded and safety rules commonly forbid recursion and require loops with a provable upper bound. // util.h using namespace std; // injected into every file that includes util.h What is wrong? It pollutes the global namespace of every includer and can silently change overload resolution. Rule theme: never put using namespace in a header . class Buffer { char* data_; public: Buffer(size_t n) : data_(new char[n]) {} ~Buffer() { delete[] data_; } // copy constructor and copy assignment: not declared }; What is wrong? The compiler-generated copy copies the pointer, so two objects delete the same memory (double free). Rule theme: rule of zero / rule of five . Prefer std::vector<char> and write no special members, or define or delete all five."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s4","h":"Static analysis and tooling","x":"Static analysis and tooling Rules are verified by static code analysers that read the source without running it. Examples named in industry include cppcheck (free), clang-tidy (free, with checks aligned to several guidelines) and commercial tools such as CodeSonar, Polyspace or Helix QAC that map findings to MISRA or AUTOSAR rule IDs. Layer What it catches Compiler warnings ( -Wall -Wextra -Werror ) Many beginner mistakes immediately, at no cost. Turn them on first. Static analyser in CI Rule violations, null dereferences, leaks, dead code across the whole code base Formatter ( clang-format ) Style, so nobody argues about braces in reviews Sanitisers and tests What static analysis cannot prove: run-time behaviour ( -fsanitize=address,undefined ) A clean report is not a proof Static analysers give false positives and miss things. They reduce risk; they do not replace design, review and testing."},{"f":"10-coding-rules.html","n":"Coding Rules and Safe C++","id":"s5","h":"Interview cheat sheet","x":"Interview cheat sheet What is MISRA, and how does AUTOSAR C++14 relate to it? MISRA is a family of coding guidelines for safe use of C and C++ in critical systems. AUTOSAR C++14 is a guideline for C++14 in safety-related environments, based on MISRA C++:2008 and updated for modern C++. Why would a safety standard forbid recursion? Stack depth cannot be bounded statically, so overflow is possible; an iterative version with a bounded loop can be analysed. Why avoid dynamic allocation in embedded real-time code? Allocation time is unpredictable, the heap can fragment, and allocation can fail at run time. Allocate once at start-up or use fixed-size pools. What is the difference between a coding guideline and a code style? Guidelines restrict how language features are used to avoid errors. Style fixes the appearance of the code: indentation, brace placement, naming. You must break a rule. What do you do? Record a justified deviation through the project's process (comment plus tool suppression referencing it), get it reviewed, and keep it as narrow as possible."}];
